  "question": {
    "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>64</mn></math> identical drops each charged upto potential of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mV</mi></math>&nbsp;are combined to form a bigger drop. The potential of the bigger drop will be _____ <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>mV</mi></math>.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "160",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The formula to calculate the potential&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>V</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;of each tiny drop is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>When all the drops forms the bigger drop the radius of the new drop&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>'</mo></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;can be calculated as follows:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnspacing=\"0px\" columnalign=\"right center left\"><mtr><mtd><mi>Volume</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>big</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>drop</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>64</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mi>volume</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>of</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>each</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>tiny</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>drop</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd/><mtd><mo>&#8658;</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>r</mi><msup><mo>'</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mi>&#960;</mi><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd/><mtd><mo>&#8658;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>r</mi><mo>'</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>So, the potential of the new big drop <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>'</mo></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;can be written as</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnspacing=\"0px\" columnalign=\"right center left\"><mtr><mtd><mi>V</mi><mo>'</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>64</mn><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>'</mo></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd/><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mrow><mn>64</mn><mi>q</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd/><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>16</mn><mi>k</mi><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Divide equation (2) by equation (1) and simplify to obtain the potential of the new drop.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnspacing=\"0px\" columnalign=\"right center left\"><mtr><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>'</mo></mrow><mi>V</mi></mfrac></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn><mi>k</mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfrac><mi>q</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mfrac></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd/><mtd><mo>&#8658;</mo></mtd><mtd><mi>V</mi><mo>'</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn><mi>V</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>3</mn></mfenced></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p><p>Substitute the value of the known parameter into equation (3) to calculate the required value of the potential for the new drop.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable columnspacing=\"0px\" columnalign=\"right center left\"><mtr><mtd><mi>V</mi><mo>'</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>16</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mV</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd/><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>160</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mV</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></p>",
